nd(ws, { type: 'error', message: 'Operation out of bounds' });\n    return;\n}\n```\nGood.\n\nFor the \"Server remains stable after malformed input\" test:\n```ts\nclient.ws.send('garbage');\n```\nThis catches in try-catch, sends error. Then checks if server still works.\n\nGood, the error handling seems fine.\n\nNow let me also think about the `stop` function in server.ts:\n\n```ts\nasync function stop(): Promise<void> {\n    return new Promise((resolve) => {\n      // Close all WebSocket connections\n      for (const client of wss.clients) {\n        client.close();\n      }\n      wss.close(() => {\n        server.close(() => {\n          db.close();\n          resolve();\n        });\n      });\n    });\n}\n```\n\nThere might be issues with this - for example, if `server.close()` is called with active connections, it might not close cleanly. But the ws library should handle it.\n\nOne concern: `wss.close()` is called immediately, but the clients haven't had time to close yet. The classic pattern is:\n\n```ts\nwss.close(() => {\n  server.close(() => {\n    db.close();\n    resolve();\n  });\n});\nfor (const client of wss.clients) {\n  client.close();\n}\n```\n\nThe `wss.close()` stops accepting new connections, then the callback fires after existing connections close. And `server.close()` waits for the HTTP server to close.\n\nActually, I think the order matters. `wss.close()` should be called first (stops accepting), then close each client connection. But `wss.close()` callback waits for all connections to close. So:\n\n```ts\nfor (const client of wss.clients) {\n  client.terminate(); // force close\n}\nwss.close(() => {\n  server.close(() => {\n    db.close();\n    resolve();\n  });\n});\n```\n\nUsing `terminate()` is more aggressive than `close()`. But for clean shutdown, `close()` is better. Let me use `close()` with a short timeout fallback.\n\nActually, looking at what the tests need:\n1. Persistence test: stop server, start new one with same db file\n2. The stop should close all WS connections\n\nLet me use a simpler approach:\n\n```ts\nasync function stop(): Promise<void> {\n    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n      // First close all WebSocket connections\n      for (const client of wss.clients) {\n        client.close();\n      }\n      // Then close the WebSocket server\n      wss.close(() => {\n        // Then close the HTTP server\n        server.close(() => {\n          db.close();\n          resolve();\n        });\n      });\n    });\n}\n```\n\nOK, I think I have a good plan now. Let me proceed with the implementation.\n\nOh wait, one more issue I want to check. Looking at the test for \"Client does not receive own operations back as broadcast\":\n\n```ts\nit('Client does not receive own operations back as broadcast', async () => {\n    const client = await createWsClient(port, docId);\n    await waitForMsg(client, 0); // sync\n    sendOp(client, { type: 'insert', position: 0, text: 'Self' });\n    const msg = await waitForMsg(client, 1);\n    // Should get ack, not operation broadcast\n    expect(msg.type).toBe('ack');\n    await closeClient(client);\n});\n```\n\nAfter sending an operation, the client expects an `ack` as message index 1. Looking at ws.ts:\n\n```ts\n// Acknowledge to sender\nsend(ws, { type: 'ack', version: newVersion });\n\n// Broadcast to other clients in the room\nbroadcastToRoom(docId, { type: 'operation', operation: transformedOp }, client);\n```\n\nThe ack is sent directly to the sender. The broadcast excludes the sender (because `client` is passed as exclude). So the sender only gets `ack` as the next message. \n\nNow for \"Client A inserts, Client B deletes\":\n\n```ts\nit('Client A inserts, Client B deletes: both changes applied', async () => {\n    const res = await request(app.app).post('/docs').send({ title: 'InsertDelete', content: 'Hello World' });\n    // ...\n    // Client 1 inserts at beginning\n    sendOp(c1, { type: 'insert', position: 0, text: 'Hey ' }, version);\n    await waitForMsg(c1, 1); // ack\n    await waitForMsg(c2, 1); // broadcast\n    // Client 2 deletes \"World\" (now at position 10 because \"Hey \" shifted it; \"Hey Hello World\")\n    sendOp(c2, { type: 'delete', position: 10, count: 5 }, version + 1);\n    await waitForMsg(c2, 2); // ack\n```\n\nAfter c1's insert at version 0: content is \"Hey Hello World\" (15 chars), version = 1.\nAfter c2 receives the broadcast at index 1.\nc2 sends delete at position 10 count 5 with version 1.\nc2's client version = 1, server's current version = 1, pendingOps = [{insert, 0, 'Hey '}] (1 element).\nopsSinceVersion: indices where baseVersion > 1. With `>`: base version of index 0 = 1 - 1 + 0 = 0 > 1? No. No ops selected.\nWith `>=`: 0 >= 1? No. Same result.\n\nSo no transformation. Delete at position 10 count 5 from \"Hey Hello World\" (15 chars): removes \"World\", leaves \"Hey Hello \". \n\nBut wait, the current delete op was sent with `version + 1` which equals the server's version. Since the pendingOps filter only selects ops AFTER the client version, and the only pending op has \"base version\" 0 (which is NOT after version 1), no transform happens. But should it?\n\nThe client sent `version: version + 1 = 1`, which is the version the client believes it's at. But the client received the broadcast of c1's operation at index 1 (in c2's messages), which means c2 knows about c1's operation. So c2 is actually at version 1 (it received the sync at version 0, then received the broadcast of the op that took it to version 1). And c2 sends the delete with version 1 (the version it knows about). So c2's delete is based on knowledge of version 1, which includes c1's operation. Therefore, c2's position 10 is correct for the content after c1's edit.\n\nSince c2 is already up-to-date (version 1 = current version 1), no transformation is needed. The delete at position 10 count 5 is correct.\n\nOK so the `>` vs `>=` issue doesn't matter here. But it matters for the \"Concurrent edits